    "trade": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "ink",
        "layout": "boxes",
        "numbered": true,
        "eyebrow": "The trade",
        "heading": "Weight and bulk, honestly",
        "lede": "<p>A waterproof sock has three layers where a racing sock has one. There’s no version of this where the weight is the same, so here’s what the difference actually is.</p>",
        "mirror": false
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Bulk is the more real objection",
            "body": "<p>They’re about the volume of a cushioned training sock. In a race-fit shoe with no room, that matters. In a normal training shoe it doesn’t.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What you’re trading it for",
            "body": "<p>Dry feet at mile ten in December instead of soaked feet at mile two. Whether that’s worth three layers is a question only your local weather can answer.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2"
      ]
    },
    "whennot": {
      "type": "honest-limits",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "ink",
        "anchor_id": "when-not",
        "eyebrow": "When not to",
        "heading": "When these are the wrong sock",
        "lede": "<p>Every brand tells you when to wear their product. On a running page that’s close to useless, because the conditions these suit are narrower than the conditions people run in.</p>",
        "footnote": "Cold and wet is where these earn their place. That’s most of a British winter. It isn’t July.",
        "cta_link": "/products/hydrosox-waterproof-socks",
        "link_url": "/pages/technology"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "m1": {
          "type": "limit",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Warm and wet",
            "body": "<p>Summer rain is the worst case. The air is already saturated, you’re producing heat, and the sweat has nowhere to go. Wear a thin sock and accept wet feet.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m2": {
          "type": "limit",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Hard efforts in mild weather",
            "body": "<p>Intervals, tempo, anything above steady in double figures. You’ll out-produce the waterproof layer within twenty minutes and finish damp from the inside.</p>"
          }
        },
        "m3": {
          "type": "limit",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "A race-fit shoe with no room",
            "body": "<p>If your racing shoe is already snug in a thin sock, don’t put a three-layer sock in it. This is a training and long-run product first.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "m1",
        "m2",
        "m3"
      ]
    },
    "winter": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "cards",
        "numbered": false,
        "eyebrow": "Winter",
        "heading": "The runs these are for",
        "lede": "<p>Not performance. Consistency — the training block surviving the months when it usually doesn’t.</p>",
        "mirror": false
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Wet feet get cold fast",
            "body": "<p>A soaked foot at five degrees loses heat quickly, and cold toes are why a lot of winter long runs get cut short. Dry feet stay warm at temperatures where wet ones don’t.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Trail, crossings and standing water",
            "body": "<p>On a wet trail you stop picking your line around the puddles, which is faster and a good deal more enjoyable.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "The pair waiting for tomorrow",
            "body": "<p>They air dry rather than tumble dry, which takes longer. If you run four mornings a week in winter you need more than one pair — that’s the honest reason the two-pair price exists.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3"
      ]
    },
    "blisters": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "wash",
        "layout": "split",
        "numbered": false,
        "eyebrow": "Blisters",
        "heading": "Blisters at running pace",
        "lede": "<p>Running compresses the same problem walkers have into a much shorter time, which is why runners feel it more sharply.</p>",
        "mirror": false
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Wet skin is weaker skin",
            "body": "<p>Soft, wet skin gives up under friction far faster. Keeping your foot dry removes one of the three things a blister needs, and at running cadence that’s the one that changes quickest.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Fit does the rest",
            "body": "<p>A sock that bunches at the toe will blister you at any moisture level. Take the bigger size if you’re between two, and check for rucking before you set off.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "We don’t guarantee this",
            "body": "<p>Some brands offer a blister-free guarantee. We don’t, because blistering depends on your shoe, your gait and your distance far more than on your sock.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3"
      ]
